  "query_preview": "..Quiet weather conditions this afternoon across \nsoutheast Arizona. Very few clouds except a few puffy cumulus clouds \nover the higher terrain mainly east of Tucson from some lingering \nmid-level moisture. As we head into the weekend, the overall pattern \nwill shift from the troughiness associated with the upper level \ndisturbance that forced recent moisture and heavy rain in our area \nto more of a ridge of high pressure building over the Desert \nSouthwest and into the Great Basin. The ridge will result in a \nslight warming and drying trend, although temperatures are forecast \nto remain up to 5 degrees below normal for early October for most \nlocales outside of Cochise County where cooler air will settle in as \na backdoor cold front moves in over the weekend allowing our eastern \nvalleys to cool into the mid to upper 70s.\n\nThe backdoor cold front will also result in breezy to locally windy \nconditions with the surface pressure gradient starting to tighten \nduring the day Friday and starting to relax a bit late Monday night. \nBig picture is we're generally looking at sustained easterly winds \nremaining below advisory criteria or around 15 to 25 mph with wind \ngusts around 30 to 35 mph. What this equates to is minor nuisance \ntype of impacts. Considering the time of year, potential nuisance \nimpacts might include your patio furniture and decorations get \nblowing around. Might be a good idea to keep an eye on the trash \ncans as well. While my confidence is low on this scenario, I think \nit is important to at least note that brief localized gusts as high \nas 50 mph cannot be ruled out mainly across the Upper Gila Valley \nnear Safford and along the State Route 83 corridor. Sudden gusts of \nthis magnitude would certainly affect high profile vehicles.\n\nOtherwise, the warming and drying trend looks to continue through \nthe middle of next week as a ridge of high pressure sticks around. \nAbove normal temperatures on the horizon with temperatures possibly \npopping back up to 100 degrees for central and western Pima County \nby next weekend and highs in the upper 80s to low 90s for other \nlocales. For early October this equates to minor to locally moderate \nHeatRisk affecting individuals who are rather sensitive to the\nheat.",
